mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of filming yourself in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nDirectional figures — attribute, mark **verify-quarterly**. The principle (presence beats polish; audio beats\nimage; the phone is enough) is stable; the exact numbers move.\n\n## Gear is not the bottleneck\n- **Any recent phone out-resolves the feed.** A 2023+ phone shoots 4K that exceeds what any social platform\n  serves back; the feed compresses everything to roughly the same ceiling. Big-name creators shoot flagship\n  videos on phones on purpose — the \"camera upgrade\" is almost never what a stalled channel needs.\n- **Back camera > front camera** on image quality on most phones, but the front camera lets you monitor framing.\n  House call: front camera until framing is muscle memory, then back camera with a mark on the floor/desk.\n- **Shoot 4K if storage allows** — it buys punch-in room in the edit (a fake second camera angle from one take).\n- **Stability is free:** a $20 tripod, or the phone leaned against a stack of books. Handheld walking shots are\n  a style, not an accident.\n\n## Audio decides perceived quality\n- **Viewers forgive soft video and never forgive bad audio** (creator consensus across platforms; attribute).\n  Echoey rooms, keyboard clatter, and wind read as amateur faster than any image flaw.\n- The fix stack, cheapest first: **quiet room with soft stuff in it** (curtains, sofa, closet-adjacent) →\n  **phone mic within ~60cm of the mouth** → **$15–25 wired lav** → wireless lav only when moving. A $300 shotgun\n  mic in an echoey room loses to a wired lav in a bedroom.\n- Clap once at the top of each take: an audible/visual sync + take marker for the edit.\n\n## Framing & light (the two-minute setup)\n- **Lens at eye level.** Below-eye reads as looming nostril-cam; slightly above is flattering; eye level is\n  honest and conversational. Stack books under the tripod before buying anything.\n- **Face the biggest window.** Window behind you = silhouette; window to the side = moody but workable; window\n  in front = free softbox. Overhead room lights alone carve eye shadows — turn them off if the window is enough.\n  After dark: one soft lamp behind the phone, aimed at the face.\n- **9:16 vertical for social; eyes in the top third; arm's-length-plus distance** (head + shoulders + hands when\n  they gesture). Keep the bottom ~25% and top ~15% clear for captions and platform UI — check\n  platform-specs-and-validation for current safe zones.\n- **Background: clean but real.** Depth behind you (room, not wall), no clutter that outcompetes your face, no\n  sterile white void. A bookshelf, a plant, a workshop — context is a trust signal.\n\n## Delivery numbers coaches actually use\n- **Energy reads ~20% flatter on camera than it feels** (long-standing broadcast coaching convention;\n  attribute). Deliver at what feels slightly too much; it lands as normal.\n- **Take quality peaks early.** Whole-video take quality typically peaks by take 2–3, then decays — energy\n  falls faster than delivery improves. Retake per beat, not per video, to reset the clock.\n- **Batch sessions fade after ~60–90 minutes.** Plan 4–8 short scripts per session and stop while the reads are\n  still alive.\n- **Reading is visible.** Eyes tracking a prompter far from the lens, or scrolling fast, telegraph \"read.\" Keep\n  prompter text in a narrow column next to the camera dot, scroll slow, rehearse twice — or drop the prompter\n  for a beat map. descript's Eye Contact AI can patch residual eye-drift; it cannot add life to a flat read.\n- **The first 3 seconds are a delivery problem too.** The script (short-form-video-script) wins them on paper;\n  on the day, the killers are the settle-breath, the chair adjust, and the warm-up sentence. Start already\n  talking, trim to the first strong word.\n\n## Wardrobe (30 seconds of thought, once)\nSolid mid-tone colors beat fine patterns (moiré) and beat logo tees (dated fast, sometimes demonetized).\nContrast with the background so you don't dissolve into it. Batch days: swap tops between scripts so a month of\nposts doesn't look like one afternoon — because it was.\n\n## House metrics\n3-second hold, average view duration / completion, and \"did they sound like themselves\" (the human's call) —\nread natively / via analytics-and-reporting; never fabricated.\n"
